  <!-- Headline -->
  <h2>What Actually Makes Airalo Different</h2>
  <p>
    Airalo sells data by the gigabyte, not unlimited. That's the opposite bet from <a href="/holafly/">Holafly</a>: instead of paying more upfront so you never think about a data meter, you pay less upfront and have to actually track what you're using. For a light user on a short trip &mdash; maps, messaging, some browsing &mdash; that's a non-issue and it's usually the cheaper choice. For a family running navigation, video calls, and a kid's tablet at the same time, the GB tracking becomes real mental overhead on top of everything else you're juggling on a trip.
  </p>
  <p>
    Airalo does also sell <strong>unlimited regional eSIMs</strong> (Europe, Asia, North America, Latin America, the Caribbean, MENA, Africa, and Australia) so it's not purely a GB-capped operation. The catch, spelled out in Airalo's own fair-use policy: unlimited plans run at full speed for the first 3GB of usage each day, then drop to 1Mbps until midnight, when the allowance resets. That's a real number worth knowing before you assume "unlimited" means unthrottled &mdash; it's still meaningfully better than a hard data cap, just not limitless.
  </p>

